	<!-- ============================= CIRCART ============================= -->
	<item>
		<title>CIRCArt</title>
		<link>https://j.langschwerts.de/projects/circart/</link>
		<pubDate>Tue, 08 Sep 2026 20:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[julian]]></dc:creator>
		<guid isPermaLink="false">https://j.langschwerts.de/?page_id=1005</guid>
		<description></description>
		<content:encoded><![CDATA[
<!-- wp:paragraph {"fontSize":"large"} -->
<p class="has-large-font-size">Place masterpieces of art history in the right order, and find out how much you actually know about the last seven hundred years of painting.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:buttons -->
<div class="wp-block-buttons"><!-- wp:button -->
<div class="wp-block-button"><a class="wp-block-button__link wp-element-button" href="/circart/">Play CIRCArt</a></div>
<!-- /wp:button --></div>
<!-- /wp:buttons -->

<!-- wp:spacer {"height":"32px"} -->
<div style="height:32px" aria-hidden="true" class="wp-block-spacer"></div>
<!-- /wp:spacer -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How it works</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>You are dealt one artwork at a time and have to slot it into a growing timeline. Get the position right and it stays; get it wrong and you see where it actually belonged. Before you place a card you can also guess the artist, the title or the exact year for bonus points &#8212; typos are forgiven, so you do not need to spell Toulouse-Lautrec correctly under pressure.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>After each round the card opens up: movement, medium, where the work hangs today, and a short piece of writing about what makes it worth knowing. That part is the actual point of the game. The timeline is the excuse.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Built with</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Plain HTML, CSS and JavaScript with no framework, so it loads fast and runs offline once cached. The card set is assembled by a Python pipeline that pulls metadata and open-access images from museum APIs &#8212; the Metropolitan Museum of Art, the Cleveland Museum of Art and Europeana &#8212; and resolves them against Wikidata. A Capacitor wrapper builds the same code into Android and iOS apps.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->

<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Image credits and rights</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->

<!-- wp:paragraph -->
<p>Every card names its artist, title, year and the collection that holds the work. The images are reproductions of artworks used here for a non-commercial educational game. If you hold rights in a work shown in CIRCArt and would like it removed or credited differently, please <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a> and I will act on it promptly.</p>
<!-- /wp:paragraph -->
]]></content:encoded>
